course: dictionary
lesson: 13530
title: Impervious: keeping things out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impervious describes something that water, air or anything else cannot get through. «The tent is made of impervious fabric, so the rain stays outside.» The rain stays outside, so the fabric must keep water out.
explain: It can also mean not affected or bothered by something. «Ben was impervious to teasing, so he just smiled and carried on.» Ben was not upset by the teasing, so impervious means not bothered by it.
explain: Check the clues around the word. In the first example the rain stays outside, and in the second Ben just smiles. Swap in "keeps water out" or "not bothered" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Impervious means that something does not let water, air or other things pass through.
fact: Clay, plastic and concrete are impervious, so water cannot soak into them.
fact: When rain falls on an impervious surface, the water cannot soak in, so puddles and floods can form.
fact: «Impervious» can also mean not affected by something, like «impervious to criticism» or «impervious to the cold».
fact: The word «impermeable» means the same as impervious in science, and «permeable» means the opposite: water can pass through.
exercise: «The tent is made of impervious nylon, so the rain stays outside.» What does impervious mean here? | *Does not let water pass through | Full of holes | Very bright | Lets water through easily
exercise: «Ben was impervious to Sam's teasing and just smiled.» What does impervious to teasing mean? | Hurt by the teasing | Very angry about it | *Not affected or bothered by it | Scared of Sam
exercise: Which of these is an impervious material? | Sponge | Paper towel | Sand | *Plastic
test: «The concrete path is impervious, so the rain runs off it.» Why do puddles form on it? | Because the rain is very heavy | Because the path is made of wood | Because concrete is soft and lets water soak in | *Because the water cannot soak into the concrete
test: «Lily was impervious to the cold, even on the snowy hill.» What does impervious to the cold mean? | Shivering all the time | Frozen solid | *Not bothered by the cold | Wearing a warm hat
exam: «The rocks are ___ to water, so the rain cannot soak in.» Which word completes the sentence? | nervous | previous | curious | *impervious
